---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CrisisModal from '../components/CrisisModal.jsx';
---

<Layout title="What Happened on February 26th | When Culture Meets AI">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="pt-10 md:pt-20 pb-12 md:pb-16 px-4 md:px-12 animate-fade-in relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none" style="background-image: url('/Poutama.svg'); background-size: 600px 600px; background-repeat: repeat; background-position: center; opacity: 0.06;"></div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto relative z-10">
      <p class="text-sm font-bold text-ako uppercase tracking-widest mb-4">February 26, 2026</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-6xl font-bold text-whenua mb-4 md:mb-6 leading-tight">When Culture Meets AI</h1>
      <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-whenua/70 leading-relaxed italic">
        Mā te kōrero ka ora — Through conversation, there is life
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Reflection CTA Banner + Slides -->
  <section class="px-4 md:px-12 pb-8">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
      <a href="/reflections" class="flex-1 block bg-ako/10 border border-ako/30 p-4 md:p-5 rounded-lg hover:bg-ako/20 transition-colors group">
        <p class="font-bold text-whenua">Haven't shared your reflections yet?</p>
        <p class="text-whenua/70 text-sm mt-1">Your post-wānanga reflections help shape this research. It only takes 5 minutes.</p>
        <span class="inline-block mt-2 text-ako text-sm font-medium group-hover:underline">Share your reflections →</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/When%20Culture%20Meets%20AI.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="sm:w-48 block bg-white/60 border border-kakahu/20 p-4 md:p-5 rounded-lg hover:border-ako/40 transition-colors group text-center sm:text-left flex flex-col justify-center">
        <p class="font-bold text-whenua">Wānanga Slides</p>
        <p class="text-whenua/70 text-sm mt-1">View the slides from the evening.</p>
        <span class="inline-block mt-2 text-ako text-sm font-medium group-hover:underline">Open PDF →</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The Wānanga -->
  <section class="py-10 md:py-16 px-4 md:px-12 border-t border-whenua/10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-base md:text-lg text-whenua/80 leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-whenua">The Wānanga</h2>

      <p>
        On Thursday 26 February 2026, co-researchers Lee Palamo and Lian Passmore hosted <em>When Culture Meets AI: Exploring Tapu, Noa, and Conversational AI</em> — an online wānanga that brought together a room of people willing to sit in questions nobody has mapped out yet.
      </p>

      <p>
        The evening was part of both Lee's and Lian's Masters of Technological Futures research at AcademyEX. Lian is building an AI relationship coach. Lee is building an AI to help people learn te reo Māori. They came to the same question from completely different directions: what happens when something vulnerable meets something digital?
      </p>

      <p>
        The people who would benefit most from these tools — people carrying shame, people who are scared, people who don't want to be judged — are the same people who are most hesitant to use them. So this wānanga wasn't about technology. It was about trust, safety, and the space between us.
      </p>

      <p>
        The session opened with a karakia from Lee, an acknowledgement of he whakaputanga and te tiriti o Waitangi, and the whakapapa of the work — the kaumātua, the kuia, the ancestors who carried knowledge forward so we could be here asking these questions.
      </p>

      <p>Three guidelines set the tone for the evening:</p>

      <div class="space-y-4 pl-4 border-l-2 border-ako/30">
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-whenua">Your feelings are data.</p>
          <p>Discomfort, hope, suspicion, curiosity — all of it mattered.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-whenua">Speak from your experience.</p>
          <p>Not what's theoretically correct. What you've actually felt, seen, or lived.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-whenua">It's okay not to know.</p>
          <p>Not knowing was welcome in this room.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What You Told Us -->
  <section class="py-10 md:py-16 px-4 md:px-12 border-t border-whenua/10 bg-white/40 backdrop-blur-sm">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-base md:text-lg text-whenua/80 leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-whenua">What You Told Us Before We Started</h2>

      <p>
        In the days leading up to the wānanga, participants had a one-on-one conversation with an AI agent built using Lian's cloned voice, or completed a written survey. These pre-conversations explored what it's like to share something personal in digital spaces, how people feel about AI holding vulnerable conversations, and where the lines are between what should be shared and what should stay protected.
      </p>

      <p>The responses ranged widely.</p>

      <p>
        Some people described the experience as surprisingly natural. One person noticed mid-conversation that they'd started talking to the AI like a real person — putting their dog outside and apologising for the interruption — then laughed at themselves for it. Another said the AI's listening and reflection skills made it feel more personal than expected.
      </p>

      <p>
        Others found it confronting. One participant described the experience as "one-sided" — uncertain whether the AI truly understood. Another drew a hard line: they would never share personal information with AI, and the idea of doing so made them cringe.
      </p>

      <p>
        And then some participants landed somewhere in between, holding both the anxiety and the excitement at once. The fear of getting it wrong alongside the pull toward connection. The recognition that AI lacks human emotion, but also the acknowledgement that sometimes, not having another human in the conversation is precisely what makes it easier to speak freely.
      </p>

      <p>
        One person put it this way: that with AI, you can have "filterless conversation" — no managing someone else's reactions, no worrying about burdening them, just unfiltered processing.
      </p>

      <p>
        On the question of tapu and noa — whether AI tends to take something sacred and turn it into just data — responses were deeply personal and often contradictory. Several participants said they hadn't thought about it in those terms and needed time to sit with it. One person spoke about the decontextualising nature of data — how numbers strip away the circumstances that give information meaning. Another pointed out that even among humans, understanding what is tapu and what is noa is something you have to learn and practise.
      </p>

      <p class="bg-white/60 border border-kakahu/20 p-5 rounded-lg italic text-whenua">
        And one voice stopped us in our tracks. A participant shared that the bigger risk isn't sharing sacred knowledge with technology — the bigger risk is not sharing it. Their whānau lost generations of knowledge because it was deemed too tapu to pass on. Their children and grandchildren will never know what their great-grandparents knew, because it died with them. Their words: "Knowledge is useless if not given to the next generation."
      </p>

      <p>
        The pre-conversations also surfaced strong views on what builders of these systems need to get right. Participants talked about transparency — knowing where data goes and what it's used for. They talked about authenticity — holding the whole story, not picking and pulling pieces. They talked about the mana of the person, and the importance of not reshaping someone's story for your own purposes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What We Explored Together -->
  <section class="py-10 md:py-16 px-4 md:px-12 border-t border-whenua/10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-10 text-base md:text-lg text-whenua/80 leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-whenua">What We Explored Together</h2>

      <p>The wānanga moved through three layers of conversation, each one going deeper.</p>

      <!-- Layer 1 -->
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold text-whenua">Your Experience With AI This Week</h3>

        <p>
          Participants were asked to describe their AI conversation in one or two words. The responses that came through the chat captured the full range: "grounding," "natural," "weird," "easy," "fluid," "unfiltered," "understood," "awkward at first then it grew on me," "artificial," "clarifying."
        </p>

        <p>
          In breakout rooms, people compared their experiences. Some found the voice-based AI conversation surprisingly comfortable — the familiarity of the cloned voice made it feel personal, like talking to someone you know. Others noticed the AI's accent shifting when it tried to pronounce te reo Māori words, which pulled them out of the experience. One group talked about how knowing who built the AI made them more willing to open up — and how that raises its own questions about trust and bias.
        </p>

        <p>
          A participant connected it to the COVID experience — the sudden shift to online learning that invaded whānau privacy, with kids trying to read on Zoom while parents scrambled to get dressed in the background. The point being that technology often arrives before tikanga has a chance to establish appropriate boundaries.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Layer 2 -->
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold text-whenua">What Feels Right About AI in Vulnerable Spaces — And What Doesn't?</h3>

        <p>
          The second round of breakout rooms widened the lens. Participants imagined someone in their whānau sitting down with AI to talk about something that really matters — a relationship, learning te reo, processing grief.
        </p>

        <p>Two themes emerged in tension with each other:</p>

        <div class="space-y-6 pl-4 border-l-2 border-ako/30">
          <div>
            <p class="font-bold text-whenua mb-2">AI as a space of safety and possibility.</p>
            <p>Several people described the value of being able to speak without managing another person's emotional response — an "infinite space holder" that doesn't get tired of you, doesn't judge you, is always available. For people experiencing shame around their relationship or their cultural knowledge, this absence of human judgment was described as genuinely liberating.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-bold text-whenua mb-2">AI as fundamentally limited.</p>
            <p>Others said that vulnerability is coupled with emotion, and AI doesn't have that. Grief and bereavement were named as spaces where AI should not go — where the feeling, the space, the understanding of isolation requires a human presence. There was concern about vulnerable people becoming over-reliant on AI, using it as a singular interaction rather than one tool among many.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          The room held both of these truths at the same time. As one group noted, AI is safer because it's not a person — and inadequate because it's not a person. Both things are true. The question isn't which one wins. It's how you hold them together.
        </p>

        <p>
          Another concern raised was transparency about where AI gets its information. When it gives advice about te ao Māori, tikanga, or cultural matters — what data is it drawing from? Who decided those sources were authoritative? Without that transparency, trust remains fragile.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Layer 3 -->
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold text-whenua">Who Gets to Decide What AI Should Hold?</h3>

        <p>
          The final question was explored as a whole room — no breakout rooms, everyone hearing each other.
        </p>

        <p>
          This was where the conversation got most real. Participants grappled with the question of who should be at the table when decisions are made about AI and cultural knowledge — and who's currently missing.
        </p>

        <p>
          One participant spoke about whakapapa being already available online whether communities wanted it there or not. You can Google whakapapa charts. That information is already out there. So the question of who "decides" what AI holds is, in some ways, already behind us. The data is already loose.
        </p>

        <p>
          Another participant flipped the narrative. Instead of seeing AI as a threat to cultural knowledge, they asked: what if it's actually a tool for preservation? Māori have always been oral people — handing knowledge down through speech. AI could be a new form of that oral tradition. If the alternative is knowledge dying with the last person who holds it, then using technology to carry it forward might be the more culturally aligned choice.
        </p>

        <p>
          Others raised the importance of Māori-led and Pasifika-led governance — that if cultural knowledge is going to live inside technology, it should be governed by the communities it belongs to, not by the companies that built the platforms.
        </p>

        <p>
          And one participant drew a parallel to bioethics — pointing out that the meaning of tapu itself evolves over time, just as debates around gene editing and xenotransplants have shifted. What was once considered untouchable may, with careful governance and community conversation, find new forms that serve the next generation.
        </p>

        <p>
          The room didn't reach a resolution. That was the point. As Lian put it: "Is that a tension we try to resolve — or a tension we learn to hold?"
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Mid-page Reflection CTA -->
  <section class="py-8 md:py-10 px-4 md:px-12 bg-ako/10 border-y border-ako/20">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <p class="font-bold text-whenua text-lg mb-2">What's sitting with you after reading this?</p>
      <p class="text-whenua/70 text-sm mb-4">If you haven't shared your post-wānanga reflections yet, we'd love to hear from you.</p>
      <a href="/reflections" class="inline-block bg-whenua text-rauhuia px-6 md:px-8 py-3 font-bold hover:bg-papa transition-colors">
        Share your reflections →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The Paradox -->
  <section class="py-10 md:py-16 px-4 md:px-12 border-t border-whenua/10 relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute -top-10 -right-20 w-[300px] h-[300px] md:w-[400px] md:h-[400px] pointer-events-none opacity-[0.06]" style="background-image: url('/Poutama.svg'); background-size: contain; background-repeat: no-repeat;"></div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-base md:text-lg text-whenua/80 leading-relaxed relative z-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-whenua">The Paradox at the Heart of This Work</h2>

      <p>
        The wānanga surfaced a paradox that sits at the centre of both Lee's and Lian's research.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same technology that might strip cultural knowledge of its sacredness — that might take something tapu and make it noa — is also the technology creating space for people carrying shame to engage with that knowledge for the first time.
      </p>

      <p>
        A te reo learner too whakamā to speak in front of others can practise with AI without anyone judging their pronunciation. Someone too ashamed to talk about their relationship can open up to AI in ways they told us they couldn't with a real person.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-white/60 border border-kakahu/20 p-5 md:p-8 rounded-lg space-y-4 text-whenua">
        <p>And yet, protecting knowledge can destroy it when there's no one left to pass it on.</p>
        <p>Sharing knowledge can diminish it when the context is stripped away.</p>
        <p class="font-bold">The thing that might be culturally dangerous might also be culturally healing.</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        The literature runs out here. What comes next has to come from rooms like the one we were in on Thursday night.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Where to From Here -->
  <section class="py-10 md:py-16 px-4 md:px-12 border-t border-whenua/10 bg-white/40 backdrop-blur-sm">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-base md:text-lg text-whenua/80 leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-whenua">Where to From Here</h2>

      <p>
        Lee and Lian are now in the final stages of completing their Masters research. All of the contributions from the pre-conversations, the wānanga, and the post-reflections are being carefully analysed and will form part of their final deliverables. When the research is complete, findings will be shared back with participants — because this was always a partnership, not an extraction.
      </p>

      <p>
        But the kōrero doesn't end with two students finishing their theses.
      </p>

      <p>
        The questions explored on Thursday night — about trust, safety, sovereignty, and what happens when culture meets technology — are questions that belong to everyone. They belong in your whānau conversations, in your workplaces, in your community organisations, in the places where decisions about technology are being made right now.
      </p>

      <p>
        If Thursday night told us anything, it's that these conversations are needed, and we are not alone in asking them.
      </p>

      <p>If you want to keep talking — about what came up for you, about what's sitting with you, about what you'd want to see next — please reach out:</p>

      <div class="bg-white/60 border border-kakahu/20 p-5 rounded-lg space-y-2">
        <p><strong class="text-whenua">Lian Passmore:</strong> <a href="mailto:lianpassmore@gmail.com" class="text-ako underline hover:text-ako/80 transition-colors">lianpassmore@gmail.com</a></p>
        <p><strong class="text-whenua">Lee Palamo:</strong> <a href="mailto:leepalamo275@gmail.com" class="text-ako underline hover:text-ako/80 transition-colors">leepalamo275@gmail.com</a></p>
        <p><strong class="text-whenua">Website:</strong> <a href="https://www.culturemeetsai.space" class="text-ako underline hover:text-ako/80 transition-colors">www.culturemeetsai.space</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Discovery Lab -->
  <section class="py-10 md:py-16 px-4 md:px-12 border-t border-whenua/10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <div class="bg-whenua/5 border border-kakahu/20 p-6 md:p-8 rounded-lg space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold text-whenua">Continue the Conversation — AI & Governance Discovery Lab</h2>

        <p class="text-base md:text-lg text-whenua/80 leading-relaxed">
          Fellow participant Taimor Hazou is convening a cross-sector Discovery Lab exploring AI governance in community and civic participation.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-white/60 p-4 rounded border border-kakahu/10 space-y-2 text-whenua/80">
          <p><strong class="text-whenua">When:</strong> Wednesday 5 March 2026, 7:30–9:30am NZT (Online)</p>
          <p class="text-sm">
            The session will examine where AI is already intersecting with community practice, governance gaps and legitimacy thresholds, and what conditions need to exist for AI to strengthen rather than distort community participation. This is a structured governance lab — not a product showcase — designed for substantive dialogue.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm">
            Participant numbers are limited to 15 to allow depth and diversity of contribution.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm text-whenua/60">
          If you'd like to be part of it, submit an Expression of Interest via the link below.
        </p>

        <a href="#" class="inline-block bg-ako text-white px-6 py-3 rounded font-bold hover:bg-teal-700 transition-colors">
          Register your interest →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Final Reflection CTA -->
  <section class="py-12 md:py-16 px-4 md:px-12 bg-ako/10 border-t border-ako/20">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold text-whenua">Your reflections are still open</h2>
      <p class="text-whenua/70 max-w-lg mx-auto">
        Whether you attended the wānanga or are reading this for the first time, we'd love to hear what's sitting with you. Talk to the AI or fill in the written form — it takes about 5 minutes.
      </p>
      <a href="/reflections" class="inline-block bg-whenua text-rauhuia px-8 py-4 font-bold text-lg hover:bg-papa transition-colors">
        Share your reflections →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Sign-off -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-24 px-4 md:px-6 text-center bg-whenua text-rauhuia relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none" style="background-image: url('/Poutama.svg'); background-size: 600px 600px; background-position: center; background-repeat: repeat; opacity: 0.12;"></div>
    <div class="relative z-10 space-y-4">
      <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl italic">Mā te kōrero ka ora. Through conversation, there is life.</p>
      <p class="text-lg font-bold">Ngā mihi nui,</p>
      <p class="text-lg">Lian Passmore & Lee Palamo</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-rauhuia/60">Masters of Technological Futures, AcademyEX</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Footer links -->
  <section class="py-8 md:py-12 px-4 md:px-12 border-t border-whenua/10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 sm:gap-8 text-sm md:text-base">
      <a href="/" class="text-ako font-medium hover:underline">← Home</a>
      <a href="/reflections" class="text-ako font-medium hover:underline">Share your reflections →</a>
      <a href="/about" class="text-ako font-medium hover:underline">About the research →</a>
      <a href="/privacy" class="text-ako font-medium hover:underline">Privacy & Data →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CrisisModal client:load />

</Layout>
